  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1257 PM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nJust a few clouds out there along the Canadian Border this\nafternoon, but otherwise a very nice and sunny day out there\nwith high pressure passing through. It's looking like another\ncold night tonight as high pressure migrates east. Parts of\nnorthwest Wisconsin and the tip of the Arrowhead will have a\ngood chance for frost and freezing temperatures tonight due to\nclear skies and light winds. Probabilities for temperatures\nbelow 36 degrees are pretty high (>60%) extending into inland\nparts of Bayfield/Sawyer counties, so I expanded the Frost\nAdvisory west and also added Ashland county to a Freeze Warning.\nThe window for frost and freezing temperatures will be a bit\nless than last night as clouds increase from west to east\ntonight ahead of an approaching low and upper-level trough.\n\nOn Saturday, expect rain and rain showers through the day as low\npressure passes through. The best chances will be along the\nInternational Border. While thunder isn't looking likely, there\nmay be some slight convective elements due to weak instability.\nWith PWATs hovering around an inch, there could be a few spunky\nshowers that could drop some locally moderate rainfall amounts.\nMost places should see a quarter inch or less, but locally\nhigher amounts can't be ruled out. Rain will pass through with\nclearing behind a cold front Saturday night.\n\nOn Sunday, expect breezy and cool post-frontal weather. There\ncould be some wraparound clouds and maybe a few sprinkles, but\nin general, it's looking pretty dry out there. Expect breezy\nconditions, especially in the Arrowhead, where wind gusts 20-30\nmph are expected.\n\nAnother area of high pressure settles in Sunday night, and we're\ncontinuing to expect a pretty widespread freeze out there, or at\nleast frost. Most inland areas will see temperatures reach into\nthe 28-32 degree range. Areas near Lake Superior and possibly\naround the Brainerd Lakes may be spared a freeze.\n\nOn Monday, southerly flow returns and a warming trend with\ntemperatures is expected. At this point, the forecast is\ncontinuing to trend dry despite passage of a trough sometime\naround Tuesday night. We'll probably see some highs in the 70s\nTuesday and Wednesday, then a drop in temperatures later in the\nweek.",
